apex_basket.moderator_conclusion:
Buy now. The thesis holds. We re-checked our numbers this week against the company's own regional figures after an independent second opinion challenged them. The second opinion was right that we had overstated the Americas and Asia-Pacific, but we had also subtracted a central-cost line that does not exist and used a share count that the buyback has since shrunk. Corrected, the base fair value is 930 pence, against 950 before, and about 45% above today's 643.5 pence. That is well clear of the 20% discount this market backdrop requires before we buy.

Inchcape is an asset-light distributor that earned a 29% return on capital in 2025, turns its profit into cash over a full year, and returns about 13% of its market value a year to shareholders. Its problem is one region: Asia-Pacific, chiefly Australia, overtaken by a sudden swing to Chinese electric cars. The Americas and Europe and Africa earned 84% of first-half profit and widened their margins.

Conviction stays a moderate 3, for four reasons:
- further estimate cuts are likely;
- the contract book churns more than a true franchise should;
- management itself says the big markets go direct, which caps how durable the moat is;
- higher rates make inventory funding dearer.

The value case on its own merits is a 4. It is held back from a 5 by that narrow, slowly eroding moat. The adverse case is 505 pence and the favourable case 1,345 pence. The next checkpoint is the investor webinar on 2026-11-19.

